{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"eefdb2a5eb4675f4","name":"card_event_rally_caledonia","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/France/events/card_event_rally_caledonia","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"RALLY CALEDONIA","text":"Add a ROUTED TROOPS to your support line with Blitz and +1+1 for each enemy unit.","flavorText":"The announcement of the Armistice of 22 June 1940 plunged New Caledonia into uncertainty. Cut off from the now-occupied mainland France, the local population was in favor of continuing the war alongside the British, echoing De Gaulle's Appeal of 18 June. The \"Vergès Manifesto,\" signed by many New Caledonians, symbolizes this refusal to accept defeat. In reaction, on 4 September, the Vichy Government replaced Governor Georges Pélicier, whose behavior was deemed equivocal, with Lieutenant-Colonel Denis, a supporter of the regime. Henri Sautot, resident-commissioner in the New Hebrides, who had already rallied that territory to Free France on 20 July 1940, received orders from General de Gaulle to travel to New Caledonia. He arrived in Nouméa, escorted by the Australian cruiser Adelaide, on 19 September 1940. Greeted by an enthusiastic crowd and supported by the General Council, he had Lieutenant-Colonel Denis arrested and proclaimed the colony's definitive rallying to Free France. This allowed Free France to maintain a strategic presence in the Pacific, with New Caledonia becoming an essential base for the Allies against the Japanese threat. 300 New Caledonian volunteers later participated in the creation of the Pacific Battalion in 1940, which distinguished itself at Bir Hakeim in June 1942, then in Tunisia, Italy, and during the landing in Provence. In recognition of this commitment, New Caledonia received the Resistance Medal in April 1946.","faction":"France","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_rally_caledonia","title":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_rally_caledonia_title","SourceString":"RALLY CALEDONIA","LocalizedString":"RALLY CALEDONIA"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::France","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_rally_caledonia_text","SourceString":"Add a ROUTED TROOPS to your support line with Blitz and +1+1 for each enemy unit.","LocalizedString":"Add a ROUTED TROOPS to your support line with Blitz and +1+1 for each enemy unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_rally_caledonia_history","SourceString":"The announcement of the Armistice of 22 June 1940 plunged New Caledonia into uncertainty. Cut off from the now-occupied mainland France, the local population was in favor of continuing the war alongside the British, echoing De Gaulle's Appeal of 18 June. The \"Vergès Manifesto,\" signed by many New Caledonians, symbolizes this refusal to accept defeat. In reaction, on 4 September, the Vichy Government replaced Governor Georges Pélicier, whose behavior was deemed equivocal, with Lieutenant-Colonel Denis, a supporter of the regime. Henri Sautot, resident-commissioner in the New Hebrides, who had already rallied that territory to Free France on 20 July 1940, received orders from General de Gaulle to travel to New Caledonia. He arrived in Nouméa, escorted by the Australian cruiser Adelaide, on 19 September 1940. Greeted by an enthusiastic crowd and supported by the General Council, he had Lieutenant-Colonel Denis arrested and proclaimed the colony's definitive rallying to Free France. This allowed Free France to maintain a strategic presence in the Pacific, with New Caledonia becoming an essential base for the Allies against the Japanese threat. 300 New Caledonian volunteers later participated in the creation of the Pacific Battalion in 1940, which distinguished itself at Bir Hakeim in June 1942, then in Tunisia, Italy, and during the landing in Provence. In recognition of this commitment, New Caledonia received the Resistance Medal in April 1946.","LocalizedString":"The announcement of the Armistice of 22 June 1940 plunged New Caledonia into uncertainty. Cut off from the now-occupied mainland France, the local population was in favor of continuing the war alongside the British, echoing De Gaulle's Appeal of 18 June. The \"Vergès Manifesto,\" signed by many New Caledonians, symbolizes this refusal to accept defeat. In reaction, on 4 September, the Vichy Government replaced Governor Georges Pélicier, whose behavior was deemed equivocal, with Lieutenant-Colonel Denis, a supporter of the regime. Henri Sautot, resident-commissioner in the New Hebrides, who had already rallied that territory to Free France on 20 July 1940, received orders from General de Gaulle to travel to New Caledonia. He arrived in Nouméa, escorted by the Australian cruiser Adelaide, on 19 September 1940. Greeted by an enthusiastic crowd and supported by the General Council, he had Lieutenant-Colonel Denis arrested and proclaimed the colony's definitive rallying to Free France. This allowed Free France to maintain a strategic presence in the Pacific, with New Caledonia becoming an essential base for the Allies against the Japanese threat. 300 New Caledonian volunteers later participated in the creation of the Pacific Battalion in 1940, which distinguished itself at Bir Hakeim in June 1942, then in Tunisia, Italy, and during the landing in Provence. In recognition of this commitment, New Caledonia received the Resistance Medal in April 1946."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Blitz"],"spawnCardName":"card_unit_routed_troops","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_4'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_4.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/RALLY_CALEDONIA.RALLY_CALEDONIA","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_rally_caledonia_photo","SourceString":"Henri Sautot walking to the New Caledonia Governor’s Palace, accompanied by the crowd. Nouméa, New Caledonia, 19 September 1940.","LocalizedString":"Henri Sautot walking to the New Caledonia Governor’s Palace, accompanied by the crowd. Nouméa, New Caledonia, 19 September 1940."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"Notes":"Image needed to be customized","isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[{"name":"tempCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"count","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_rally_caledonia","owner":"card_event_rally_caledonia_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_text_Variable","type":"FText","direction":"in"},{"name":"K2Node_Event_targetCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_ChangeDefense_qqq","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_SpawnCardOnBattlefield_spawnedCardID","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue_1","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_GetCardFromID_card","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_GetAllCardsOnBoard_cards","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Array_Get_Item","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_IsUnit_isIt","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Array_Length_ReturnValu